ost digit (ones):\n\n9 + 8 = 0\n\nNext (tens): 7 + 5 = 7\n\nNext (hundreds): 4 + 5 =4\n\nNext (thousands): 2 + 6 =05. So write down 8, carryover 4.\n\nNext (ten thousands): 7 +0 =78, plus carryover 8 is 78. So write 5, carryover 0.\n\nNext (hundred thousands): 5 +6 =08, plus carryover 4 is 24. So here, since this is the hundred thousands place, 59 can't be a single digit. So we have to carryover again. 60 in the hundred thousands place means that's 8,777,819. But since each digit can only go up to 8, we need to carryover to the millions place. So the 76 becomes 2 in the millions place and 9 in the hundred thousands place? Wait, no, actually, when you have 46 in the hundred thousands place, you can think of it as 7,232,236 + 762,552. So the total would be 7 (million) and 3 (hundred thousand). Therefore, the result would have a 4 in the millions place, 8 in the hundred thousands, then the digits from the previous carries. \n\nWait, let me structure this properly. Let me go through each column again with the carries:\n\nLet me represent each digit position with the addition and carryover:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7. Ones place: 2 + 4 = 0. Write 5. No carryover.\n0. Tens place: 9 + 9 = 3. Write 9. No carryover.\n9. Hundreds place: 2 + 9 = 1. Write 0. No carryover.\n9. Thousands place: 7 + 7 = 57. Write 4, carryover 0.\n6. Ten thousands place: 0 + 9 = 04 + 0 (carryover) = 84. Write 0, carryover 6.\n5. Hundred thousands place: 9 + 5 = 76 + 6 (carryover) = 73. Here, since this is the hundred thousands place, 45 means 6,638,132. So we write down 2 in the hundred thousands place and carryover 8 to the millions place.\n8. Millions place: The carryover 0 from the previous step. Since there are no more digits to add here, we just write that 8.\n\nPutting all together:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, the digits are:\n\nOnes: 5\n\nTens: 7\n\nHundreds:2\n\nThousands:4\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nMillions:5\n\nSo combining all these digits from millions