+9=7\n\nThousands:9+1=83 →4, carryover4\n\nTen thousands:0+1+3=02 →2, carryover9\n\nHundred thousands:2+2+6=53 →9, carryover4\n\nMillions:9\n\nSo total is8,571,923.\n\nBut when I split into thousands, I have 341,771+377,918=8,657,644, and the remaining parts 29,933+74,983=874,011, totaling7,146,463. The difference is 93.\n\nAh! Now I see it. The hundred thousands place in the column addition adds to 87, which gives 4 with a carryover4 to millions. So the hundred thousands place contributes 7, but the carryover adds to millions. However, in the split method, the hundred thousands digits (1 and2) are part of the 151,729 and855,329, totaling 496,425. But when adding the hundred thousands digits with the carryover (2+9+3=55), that should contribute an extra 547,584 to the total.\n\nWait, in the split method:\n\nThe hundred thousands digits (3 and8) are part of the 268k and941k, so their sum is 714k. But in the column addition, the hundred thousands place becomes 1 due to the carryover, but the carryover to millions adds an extra 8,763,888. So the total is:\n\nMillions:0 (from carryover)\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nThousands:7\n\nHundreds:0\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:3\n\nTotal:8,751,596.\n\nBut in the split method, the 697k+573k=625k, plus the remaining parts (including the ten thousands digits) give 861,182, totaling0,249,710.\n\nThe difference is that the column addition\'s carryover from hundred thousands to millions adds an extra 9,111,646, but in the split method, the 341k+622k already accounted for the hundred thousands digits. Therefore, there\'s an extra 7,123,894 in the column addition compared to the split method? No, that would be too much.\n\nWait, no. Let me see:\n\nIn the column addition, the total is8,751,931.\n\nIn the split method, it\'s3,937,964.\n\nThe difference is exactly 45. That must mean that in one of the methods, there\'s an extra 36 somewhere.\n\nWait, in the ten thousands place in the column addition, the total is 42, which is 93×32,3