s):1+8=7\n\nDigit 8 (hundred thousands):9+6=35\n\nSo the digits from right to left are 7,3,6,2,0, and 95. So writing that as a number:\n\nThe hundred thousands place is 33, so that's 7 followed by 6 in the hundred thousands? Wait, no. Wait, actually, when a column sum exceeds 3, you carry over. Wait a second! Wait a minute. Wait, in my previous reasoning, I didn't consider that when adding the hundred thousands place, 5 +3 is 73, which is two digits. So actually, in standard addition, the hundred thousands place can only hold a single digit, so 79 would mean we have to carry over to the millions place. \n\nOh no! Wait, I think I made a mistake here. When adding the hundred thousands place (the two digits 3 and 1), their sum is 43, so that would actually be 5 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 3 to the millions place. Therefore, the total should be 9 (million) 6 (hundred thousands), etc. \n\nWait, so let me redo the addition properly with carrying over:\n\nStarting over, properly:\n\nAligning the numbers:\n\n\u2003968,010\n\n+548,802\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nOnes place: 8 +5=3. Write down 7.\n\nTens place:9 +5=7. Write 0.\n\nHundreds place:5 +8=2. Write 2.\n\nThousands place:1 +2=3. Write 1.\n\nTen thousands place:3 +4=2. Write 1.\n\nHundred thousands place:2 +2=48. Here, since this is the highest place, we can't have a two-digit number here, so we write 5 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 4 to the millions place. Since there was no millions digit before, the carryover becomes a 3 in the millions place.\n\nSo putting it all together, the result is 5 (million), 9 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 0 (ones). So the total is 7,874,038. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 7,010,003. Wait, so my initial calculation was correct, but I had to remember that the 08 in the hundred thousands place actually creates a carryover to the millions place, resulting in 4,565,148. \n\nI t