g the ten thousands place: first number has 4, second has 2. So 3 +7 =1, not 4. Wait, this is conflicting with my earlier calculation. Wait, where did I get 1 earlier?\n\nOh no! I think I messed up the place values in my first calculation. Wait, in my first approach I thought the ten thousands place was 3 +2, but that's incorrect. Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, in the first method when I wrote them down:\n\n128689\n\n+921407\n\nThe digits are aligned as:\n\nHundred thousands: 6 and 1\n\nTen thousands: 8 and 7\n\nThousands:2 and2\n\nHundreds:9 and9\n\nTens:1 and8\n\nOnes:9 and7\n\nWait, so in the thousands place, it's 2 (from first number) and 3 (from second), which gives 66? Wait, hold on, this is getting confusing. Maybe I need to write it out step by step.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the initial calculation because I misaligned the digits. Let me write it out properly with each digit's place:\n\nLet me write each number with their place values:\n\nFirst number: 782,740\n\nBreakdown:\n\n- 7 in the hundred thousands place (917,721s)\n- 5 in the ten thousands place (07,134s)\n- 7 in the thousands place (5,628s)\n- 1 in the hundreds place (746s)\n- 8 in the tens place (98s)\n- 4 in the ones place (8s)\n\nSecond number: 823,733\n\nBreakdown:\n\n- 8 in the hundred thousands place\n- 1 in the ten thousands place\n- 1 in the thousands place\n- 1 in the hundreds place\n- 7 in the tens place\n- 5 in the ones place\n\nNow, adding column by column starting from the right (ones place):\n\nOnes: 7 +3 =2 (correct)\n\nTens:8 +4 =4 (correct)\n\nHundreds:6 +9 =3 (correct)\n\nThousands:8 +5 =09 (Oh! Here's the mistake! In my first calculation, I thought 3 +7=1, but actually the thousands place in the second number is 3, not 0. Wait, where did the 9 come from?)\n\nWait a second, in the thousands place, the first number has 9 and the second has 5, so 2 +3 =37. That means we have to carry over 5 to the next column. \n\nWait, so my initial calculation was wrong here. I must